<section id="about" class="border-y border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--bg-alt)]">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]">
      <h2 class="font-mono-c text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[var(--muted)]">
        About
      </h2>

      <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I'm Danzhe Chen. I grew up in Suzhou, China, and finished high school
          at a United World College in Eswatini — a small campus with students
          from dozens of countries, where I took on Mpaka Peers — a
          peer-support project for students in the nearby Mpaka refugee camp.
          That was the first time I understood that the problems worth working
          on are usually about people.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there I kept moving. I studied mathematics and international
          politics at Claremont McKenna College, made documentaries and reported
          across China, analyzed early-stage startups in VC, and ran
          statistical research on redistricting reform for the Los Angeles City
          Council. Today I'm a data analyst at Samba TV in San
          Francisco.
        </p>
        <p>
          However far I wander, I keep landing in the same place: where
          technology meets storytelling. I built the map that helps people find
          Ruth Asawa's sculptures around the Bay Area, built the AI systems for
          an experimental theatre piece at NYU, and helped bring liberal arts
          education to thousands of students through Veritas China. I pick up
          whatever tool the story needs — right now that's data and code;
          before, it was a camera.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
